					<description><![CDATA[<p>Funding Supports AI Networking Expansion Israeli data center AI infrastructure technology specialist Xsight Labs has raised over $300 million, pushing up its post-money valuation to $2.8 billion, a move which</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/xsight-labs-raises-over-300m/" data-wpel-link="internal">Xsight Labs Raises Over $300M At $2.8 Billion Valuation To Expand AI Networking Platform</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<h2><b>Funding Supports AI Networking Expansion</b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Israeli data center AI infrastructure technology specialist Xsight Labs has raised over $300 million, pushing up its post-money valuation to $2.8 billion, a move which will help product scaling and boost wider market penetration in the fast growing artificial intelligence market. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The financing was headed by a series of funds which included Fidelity Management &amp; Research Company. The rest had been completed by Aliya Capital Partners, Atreides Management, Artisan Partners, Battery Ventures, Diagonal Capital, Intel Capital among others.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The funds will enable Xsight Labs to continue developing its multi-gen roadmap for Ethernet switches and Data Processing Units (DPUs) and build on the UEC (Ultra Ethernet Consortium) roadmap. The company will hire engineers, expand manufacturing, supply chains, and customer delivery. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">&#8220;Our company is committed to delivering the highest-performance networking solutions and a distributed, smart, high-throughput network infrastructure to drive next-generation AI workloads and hyperscale cloud infrastructure,&#8221; said Xsight Labs CEO, Yossi Meyouhas.</span></p>
<h2><b>Advanced Hardware Powers AI Data Centres</b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Established in January 2020 to create bleeding-edge networking technology solutions, Xsight Labs provides intelligent connectivity technology for open ecosystems from </span><a href="https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/hyperscale" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">hyperscale </span></a><span style="font-weight: 400">cloud to the enterprise edge, AI and data centers. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The company offers products including its E1 DPU, Intelligent Processors, and X2 Data Center Switch solutions. The X2 Data Center Switch delivers 800G line-rate processing with low-latency networking and industry-leading power efficiency.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">It operates on open standards including DPDK and SONiC within Linux-based networking environments for enterprises. The platform uses a programmable data plane powered by Xsight Instruction Set Architecture compiler technology efficiently.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Xsight Labs currently operates research and development centres and regional offices across Israel, the United States, and Armenia, supporting customers building increasingly complex AI computing environments.</span></p>
<h2><b>SpaceX Collaboration Highlights Commercial Progress<br />
</b></h2>
<div id="attachment_203894" style="width: 1322px" class="wp-caption alignnone"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-203894" class="size-full wp-image-203894" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/a-futuristic-poster-showcases-a-sprawlin_srTK6iKXXB6uGsEWLpVCOw_VQEB6ZvoRSyNMbWKnLUsWg_cover.png" alt="A neon futuristic visual showing AI networking hardware powering satellite systems and cloud infrastructure." width="1312" height="736" /><p id="caption-attachment-203894" class="wp-caption-text">Xsight Labs enables programmable high-speed networks for AI systems and space-based infrastructure. Source: Created by Ventureburn</p></div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">These capabilities allow customers to customise networking infrastructure. The funding announcement follows a successful joint technology trial involving SpaceX, where Xsight processors were integrated into the company&#8217;s new V3 satellite platform.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">According to reports, the company&#8217;s programmable X2 switch enables customers to modify networking software after deployment, making it particularly valuable for space applications where remote updates are essential. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The funding reflects growing demand for networking hardware supporting large-scale artificial intelligence systems and cloud infrastructure. It also supports specialised computing environments requiring faster, scalable, and highly efficient networking technologies. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">More News: </span><a href="https://ventureburn.com/cais-raises-170m/" data-wpel-link="internal"><span style="font-weight: 400">CAIS Raises $170M At $2 Billion Valuation To Expand Alternative Investment Platform</span></a></p>
<h2><b>Investment Reflects Rising Demand For AI Infrastructure</b></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Demand for faster and more efficient networking hardware has become critical for modern data centre architecture. AI training clusters require massive bandwidth, ultra-low latency, and reconfigurable networking solutions for efficient data movement. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">These capabilities enable processing of enormous data volumes required by advanced artificial intelligence computing workloads today. Large institutional investors and technology-focused venture firms increasingly support silicon companies developing essential artificial intelligence infrastructure. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Their investments reflect growing confidence in silicon companies enabling the next generation of artificial intelligence computing. Investment capital is shifting from artificial intelligence software toward silicon companies building connectivity and computation platforms. </span></p>
