		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://ventureburn.com/?p=203883</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>K2 Space raises $500 million to beat other satellite companies in their own game. The Series D round has increased the company’s value to $6.8 billion. This is notably more</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/k2-space-raises-500m-6-8-billion-valuation/" data-wpel-link="internal">K2 Space Raises $500 Million At $6.8B Valuation To Scale Large, High-Power Satellites</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><span style="font-weight: 400">K2 Space raises $500 million to beat other satellite companies in their own game. The <a href="https://x.com/K2SpaceCo/status/2082822358268809611" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external">S</a></span><a href="https://x.com/K2SpaceCo/status/2082822358268809611" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">eries </span><span style="font-weight: 400">D round</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> has increased the company’s value to $6.8 billion. This is notably more than twice what it was worth after its Series C round in December.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Karan Kunjur is the company’s co-founder and CEO. He started the company four years ago with his brother, Neel. Their idea was quite on point: that bigger satellites would end up being the hot topic in space missions rather than smaller satellites.</span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">From Pitch Deck to Orbit</span></h2>
<div id="attachment_203885" style="width: 945px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-203885" class="size-full wp-image-203885" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/K2-Space-team.jpg" alt="K2 Space executives met with Lt. Gen. Dennis Bythewood, Commander, U.S. Space Forces – Space (S4S) at the company’s manufacturing facility in Torrance, California. Credit: K2 Space" width="935" height="545" /><p id="caption-attachment-203885" class="wp-caption-text">K2 Team &amp; Lt. Gen. Dennis Bythewood</p></div>
<p><a href="https://www.k2space.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">K2 Space</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400">’s argument has left the theoretical phase. The company has a Gravitas satellite which is already running a 20-kilowatt Hall-effect thruster on orbit. It says the satellite is very powerful. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">That would be the proof point behind the pitch: heavier satellites can handle communications needing data and defense missions that smaller platforms simply can’t. Backing that up requires 100% manufacturing power, and K2 built for it early. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The company’s factory in Torrence is a 180,000-square-foot facility, capable of producing up to 100 satellites a year. More than 85% of each satellite’s platform was built in the factory. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">This is a major reason investors think K2 can go from a handful of satellites to dozens, then hundreds, without the challenges in production that have slowed other manufacturers.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/cisco-backed-eliyan-raises-145m-at-1b-valuation/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Eliyan Raises $145M to Fix AI’s Data Bottleneck</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">The Contracts Behind the Valuation</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The Space Force picked K2 as a satellite-bus provider for its Protected Tactical Satcom-Global program, the company’s first program of record with the Pentagon. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Commercial satellite operator SES has requested an initial batch of 30 satellites for its meoSphere network. Also, defense contractor Anduril has agreed for K2 to build satellites for the Golden Dome missile-defense program.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Following proper calculations, K2 says it’s holding more than $1 billion in signed commercial and government contracts. The company’s next mission is set to launch multiple satellites in the second quarter of 2027 and a large 100-kilowatt “Giga-class” satellite in the second half of 2028.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/onyx-security-raises-113-million-series-b-ai-agent-governance/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Onyx Raises $113M To Scale Next-Gen AI Governance Models</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">K2 Space is Riding a Bigger Wave</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Space companies raised about $7.5 billion across 141 venture deals in the second quarter of this year, following a record of $8 billion across 159 deals in the first quarter.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Investors backing K2 are framing the round as a bet on infrastructure. “Every important mission in space comes back to power and mass, and K2 recognized that before anyone else,” said Lucas Oliveira, a principal at Kleiner Perkins. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">ICONIQ general partner Tengbo Li put it more broadly: “We believe space will be one of the defining frontiers of the coming century, and K2 is building the platform to power the next generation of the space economy.”</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/tech-giants-commit-265-million-on-electricians-data-centers/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Tech Giants Commit $265M To Data Center Electricians</i></b></a></p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/k2-space-raises-500m-6-8-billion-valuation/" data-wpel-link="internal">K2 Space Raises $500 Million At $6.8B Valuation To Scale Large, High-Power Satellites</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
			</item>
