<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/cisco-backed-eliyan-raises-145m-at-1b-valuation/" data-wpel-link="internal">Cisco-Backed Startup Eliyan Raises $145M at $1B Valuation to Fix AI&#8217;s Data Bottleneck</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Everyone&#8217;s watching chip speed. Almost nobody&#8217;s watching what happens between the chips and that&#8217;s exactly where the industry is losing the most performance. In dense AI clusters, GPUs typically run at just 30 to 40% utilization, which means most of the time even top-tier chips are sitting there waiting on data instead of processing it.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Cisco-backed startup </span><a href="https://eliyan.com/news/eliyan-achieves-unicorn-status-with-145-million-series-c/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">Eliyan raises $145M</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> at a $1B valuation to solve this problem. With clusters larger than a certain size, data simply can&#8217;t move quick enough. Simply shoving more of it down the ordinary copper wire consumes more power and generates more heat and ultimately slows down chips. Eliyan is solving this problem.</span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">The Problem Eliyan Actually Solves</span></h2>
<div id="attachment_203874" style="width: 810px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-203874" class="size-full wp-image-203874" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Eliyan-founders-.jpg" alt="A group photograph showing the three founders of Eliyan Ramin Farjadrad, Patrick Soheili, and Syrus Ziai" width="800" height="418" /><p id="caption-attachment-203874" class="wp-caption-text">Eliyan Founders</p></div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Eliyan doesn&#8217;t make chips. It makes the connections between them. Founded in Santa Clara by Ramin Farjadrad, Patrick Soheili, and Syrus Ziai, the company&#8217;s NuLink PHY cores move data between dies on the same package and across separately packaged chips.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Its NuGear chiplets handle memory expansion and rack-to-rack scaling. The detail that matters most for chipmakers is packaging. Competing approaches often need expensive silicon interposers to hit high bandwidth. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Eliyan&#8217;s chiplets hit similar numbers on standard organic substrates, which means a chip designer can push more throughput without redesigning the physical chip from scratch.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/onyx-security-raises-113-million-series-b-ai-agent-governance/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Onyx Raises $113M To Scale Next-Gen AI Governance Models</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">Why Cisco and Lumentum Are Backing Eliyan</span></h2>
<div id="attachment_203873" style="width: 1034px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-203873" class="size-large wp-image-203873" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Eliyan-Delivers-Industrys-Highest-Performing-Chiplet-Interconnect-PHY-at-64Gbps-in-3nm-Process-1-1024x538.jpg" alt="Eliyan’s electro-optical interconnect tech fixes data bottlenecks between AI chips." width="1024" height="538" /><p id="caption-attachment-203873" class="wp-caption-text">Eliyan Chipset</p></div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Seligman Ventures led the round with Umesh Padval, Seligman&#8217;s managing partner, joining the board of directors for Eliyan. He was a former board member at Mellanox prior to its $6.9 billion acquisition by Nvidia which changed the way the industry views networking. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Both are new strategic investors: Cisco has a spin-out dedicated to optics and silicon, and Lumentum is making the lasers and optical modules that hyperscale data centers will purchase. This comes in addition to a list of industry chip industry leaders backing Eliyan. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Before this round, AMD, Arm, Meta, Coherent, Intel Capital and Samsung Catalyst Fund had all invested in Eliyan. If anyone is investing so early, they are typically looking to be a part of what&#8217;s being constructed next and not just for a return.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/tech-giants-commit-265-million-on-electricians-data-centers/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Tech Giants Commit $265M To Data Center Electricians</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">The Scale Eliyan Is Betting On</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Eliyan plans to start delivering its first chiplets later this year and will reach hundreds of millions of dollars by 2027, the company said. It&#8217;s also sitting on more than 100 patents. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Their bet is that the interconnect is the bottleneck for chiplets, and they own the IP to solve it. That gives it breathing room as bigger players like Nvidia and Intel start paying attention to the same problem.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The next stretch of the AI race won&#8217;t be decided by whoever ships the single fastest chip. It&#8217;ll come down to who can move data across a warehouse full of them without melting the power bill, and that&#8217;s the fight Eliyan just raised $145 million to win.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/freehand-raises-75-million-ai-agents-supply-chain/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Freehand Raises $75M for AI Agents in Supply Chains</i></b></a></p>
